---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Wallpaper from '@/components/tools/calc2/Wallpaper.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="wallpaper-calculator">
  <Wallpaper client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the wallpaper calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Wallpaper is hung in vertical strips, so the wallpaper calculator counts strips, not square feet. It
      finds how many strips go around the room, how long each strip must be for the pattern to line up,
      and how many of those strips one roll gives.
    </p>
    <pre><code>strips        = ⌈(perimeter − doors) ÷ roll width⌉
strip length  = wall height + trim, rounded up to whole pattern repeats
strips a roll = ⌊roll length ÷ strip length⌋
rolls         = ⌈strips ÷ strips a roll⌉</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 12 × 10 ft room has 44 ft of wall, less 3 ft for one door: 41 ft, or 492 in. With 27 in wide
      rolls that is ⌈492 ÷ 27⌉ = 19 strips. The walls are 8 ft (96 in) high; with 4 in of trim each strip
      needs 100 in, and a 21 in repeat rounds that up to 105 in. A 33 ft (396 in) roll gives 3 strips, so
      the room needs ⌈19 ÷ 3⌉ = 7 rolls.
    </p>

    <h2>Pattern repeat</h2>
    <p>
      Every strip starts at the same point of the pattern, so a large repeat wastes paper on every strip.
      For the 100 in strips above, no repeat gives 100 in strips, a 21 in repeat 105 in and a 27 in repeat
      108 in. The calculator shows the paper left at the end of each roll.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Windows are not deducted, because the wall above and below them still takes strips. Buy one extra
      roll from the same batch: rolls from different batches can differ in colour.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
